/*
 * PracticeSection — keeps a lesson's instructions on screen while the learner
 * works in its practice terminal.
 *
 * THE PROBLEM (audit: "The persistent side-by-side terminal"). A practice
 * section is written instructions-then-terminal. Scroll down to type, and the
 * instructions leave the screen at the exact moment they are needed.
 *
 * WHY THIS IS A WRAPPER AND NOT ONE CSS RULE. `position: sticky` is bounded by
 * its containing block, so a bare rule on `.tmx-terminal` would be bounded by
 * the whole article: once pinned, the terminal would ride along over the Recap
 * and the Next/Prev cards for the rest of the page. Wrapping gives the sticky
 * element a containing block that ENDS at the section, so it releases at the
 * next heading on its own, with no scroll listener and no JS.
 *
 * WHY BOTTOM AND NOT TOP. The audit's phase 1 said `top: calc(nav + 1rem)`,
 * which cannot work here: the prose sits ABOVE the terminal, so pinning the
 * terminal to the top of the viewport lets the instructions scroll away behind
 * it — the very failure being fixed. Anchoring to the BOTTOM parks the terminal
 * against the lower edge and leaves the whole space above it for the prose,
 * which is the actual goal. True side-by-side is phase 2 and needs a wider
 * layout: the content column is 45rem, and splitting that leaves neither a
 * readable measure nor the ~55 columns the lessons' output needs.
 */
---

<div class="tmx-practice"><slot /></div>
